¶
{{smaller block|At the ''zeres'' end thei comen azen, and founden the same Lettres and Figures, the whiche thei hadde writen the ''zeer'' before, withouten ony defaute.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 17.}}}}

{{smaller block|Thei sholde not returne with-inne two ''yere'', lesse than thei myght fynde the seide childe.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), i. 29.}}}}

'''5.''' ''pl.'' Period of life; age: as, he is very vigorous for his ''years'': often used specifically to note old age. See ''in years'', below.
{{smaller block|<poem>He is made as strong as brass, is of brave ''years'' too,
And doughty of complexion.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Rule a Wife, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|He [Essex] . . . profess’d he would not contend with the Queen, nor excuse the Faults of his young ''Years'' either in whole or in part.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 389.}}}}

{{smaller block|He himselfe affected ease and quiet, now growing into ''yeares''.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, Aug. 18, 1673.}}}}

{{smaller block|What is there quite so profoundly human as an old man’s memory of a mother who died in his earlier ''years''?
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, viii.}}}}

{{smaller block|The older plural ''year'' still remains in popular language: as, the horse is ten ''year'' old.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And threescore ''year'' would make the world away.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Sonnets, xi.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Then you know a boy is an ass,
Then you know the worth of a lass,
Once you have come to forty ''year''.</poem>
{{right|''Thackeray'', Age of Wisdom.}}}}

'''Anomalistic year.''' {{smaller|See ''anomalistic''.}}—'''Astral year.''' {{smaller|Same as ''sidereal year''.}}—'''Astronomical year.''' {{smaller|See def. 1.}} —'''A year and a day,''' {{smaller|the lapse of a year with a day added to it: in law constituting a period which in some cases determines a right or liability: as, where one is fatally wounded with murderous intent, the killing is murder if death ensues within ''a year and a day''. See ''day''<sup>1</sup>.}}

