{{smaller block|Sunbeams never came, never gleamed, ''year in, year out,'' across the clear darkness of the broad water floor.
{{right|''C. F. Woolson'', East Angels, xxviii.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Year of confusion,''' the 707th year of the Roman era, ending with 47 {{sc|b. c.}}, being the year before the first introduction of the Julian calendar. It had 445 days.—'''Year of grace,''' year of the Christian era.—'''Year of jubilee.''' See ''jubilee'', 1.—'''Year of our Lord,''' year of the Christian era.—'''Years of discretion.''' See ''discretion''.—'''Young of the year.''' See ''young''.}}

¶
'''yeara,''' ''n.'' See ''poison-oak''.

'''year-bird''' (yēr′bėrd), ''n.'' The djolan: said to have been so called from a notion that it annually added a wrinkle to the plicated skin at the base of the beak.

'''year-book''' (yēr′bu̇k), ''n.'' '''1.''' A book giving facts about the year, its chief seasons, festivals, dates, etc., or other kindred subjects: as, Hone’s ''Year-Book''.—'''2.''' A book published every year, every annual issue containing new or additional information; a work published annually and intended to supply fresh information on matters in regard to which changes are continually taking place: as, a parish ''year-book''.
{{smaller block|A new ''year-book'', specially prepared for business-men, will be issued, . . . under the title of The ''Year-Book'' of Commerce.
{{right|''The Academy'', June 1, 1889, p. 376.}}}}
'''3.''' One of a number of books containing chronological reports of early cases adjudged or argued in the courts of England. {{smaller|The series first printed and long known as ''The Year Books'' contains cases from the beginning of the reign of Edward II. down to the end of Edward III., and from the beginning of Henry}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|IV. down to near the end of Henry VIII. Others later published are Maynard’s Edward I. and II., and Horwood’s translation from MS. which presents cases in various years of Edward I. from 11 to 35 inclusive.}}

¶'''yeard''', ''n.'' An obsolete or dialectal form of ''eard'' and of ''earth''.

'''year-day†''' (yēr′dā), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ȝereday'' (cf. AS. ''géardagas'', pl., days of yore); < ''year'' + ''day''<sup>1</sup>.] An anniversary day; a day on which prayers were said for the dead. ''Halliwell.''

{{smaller block|We haue ordeyned . . . to kepe the ''ȝereday'' of Jon lyster of Cambryge ȝerely, on mydelenton sonday, . . . because he gafe vs iiij Marc. in the begynnyng and to the fortheraunce of our gylde.
{{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 281.}}}}

'''yeard-fast†''', ''a.'' Fast in the earth or ground.

{{smaller block|<poem>O about the midst o’ Clyde’s water
There was a ''yeard-fast'' stane.</poem>
{{right|''Burd Ellen'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 214).}}}}

'''yeared''' (yērd), ''a.'' [< ''year'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Numbering years; aged.

{{smaller block|<poem>Both were of best feature, of high race,
''Yeared'' but to thirty. ''B. Jonson'', Sejanus, i. 1.</poem>}}

'''yearlily''' (yēr′li-li), ''adv.'' [< ''yearly'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Yearly. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The great quaking grass sowen ''yearlily'' in many of the London gardens. {{right|''T. Johnson'', Herball.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''yearling''' (yēr′ling), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [= G. ''jährling''; as ''year'' + ''-ling''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. L. ''vitulus'', a calf, lit. a ‘yearling’: see ''veal''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A young beast one year old or in the second year of its age.—'''2.''' Under racing and trotting rules, a horse one year old, dating from January 1st of the year of foaling.
{{smaller block|He was buying ''yearlings'', too, and seemed keen about racing, but as yet not a feather had been plucked from the pigeon’s wing. {{right|''Whyte Melville'', White Rose, II. vi.}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' A year old; of a year’s age, duration, or date: as, a ''yearling'' heifer.
{{smaller block|As ''yearling'' brides provide lace caps, and work rich clothes for the expected darling.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes, i.}}}}

'''yearlong''' (yēr′lông), ''a.'' Lasting or continuing a year.
{{smaller block|<poem>“Thee,” I said,
“From ''yearlong'' poring on thy pictured eyes,
Ere seen I loved.”</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Princess, vii.}}
Accepting ''year-long'' exile from his home.
{{right|''The Atlantic'', LIX. 361.}}}}

'''yearly''' (yēr′li), ''a.'' [< ME. ''yeerly'', < AS. ''geárlīc'' (= G. ''jährlich''); as ''year'' + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Annual; happening, accruing, or coming every year: as, a ''yearly'' rent or income.
{{smaller block|Five hundred poor I have in ''yearly'' pay.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iv. 1. 315.}}
These two last [Euphrates and Tigris] are famous for their ''yearely'' ouerflowings. {{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 340.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>Whose cheerful tenants bless their ''yearly'' toil.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Moral Essays, iv. 183.}}}}

'''yearly''' (yēr′li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''yerely''; < ''yearly'', ''a.''] Annually; once a year: as, blessings ''yearly'' bestowed.
{{smaller block|Also there shalbe allowed to him fower Vshers, every of them being ''yerely'' allowed for the same 20<sup>li</sup>.
{{right|''Booke of Precedence'' (E. E. T. S., extra ser.), i. 2.}}
''Yearly'' will I do this rite. {{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, v. 3. 23.}}}}

'''yearn'''<sup>1</sup> (yėrn), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''yernen'', ''ȝernen'', < AS. ''giernan'', ''gyrnan'', ''geornian'', yearn, desire, = Icel. ''girna'' = Goth. ''gairnjan'', desire, long for; from an adj., AS. ''georn'', ME. ''ȝern'' = OS. ''gern'' = OHG. MHG. ''gern'' = Icel. ''gjarn'' = Sw. ''gerna'' = Dan. ''gjærne'' = Goth. *''gairns'' (in comp. ''faihu-gairns''), desirous, eager (see ''yern''<sup>1</sup>); with formative ''-n'', from the root seen in OHG. MHG. ''ger'', eager, OHG. ''gerōn'', MHG. ''geren'', G. ''be-gehren'', long for.] '''1.''' To long for something; desire eagerly; feel desire or longing.
{{smaller block|<poem>Angels euer sese and euer thay ''ȝerne'' for to see.</poem>
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 4.}}
<poem>Drede delitable drynke, and thow shalt do the bettere;
Mesure is medcyne, thouȝ thow moche ''ȝerne''.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), i. 35.}}
<poem>O, Juvenal, lorde, trewe is thy sentence,
That litel witen folk what is to ''yerne''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 198.}}
Joseph made haste, for his bowels did ''yearn'' upon his brother. {{right|Gen. xliii. 30.}}
All men have a ''yearning'' curiosity to behold a man of heroic worth. {{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 340.}}
<poem>But my heart would still ''yearn'' for the sound of the waves
That sing as they flow by my forefathers’ graves.</poem>
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', The Hudson.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''2†.''' To cry out eagerly; give tongue, as a dog.
{{smaller block|When Foxes and Badgerds haue yong cubbes, take all your olde Terryers and put them into the grounde; and when they beginne to baye (which in the earth is called ''yearnyng''), you muste holde your yong Terryers, . . . that they may herken and heare theyr fellowes ''yearne''.
{{right|''Turberville'', Booke of Hunting (ed. 1575), p. 181.}}}}

'''yearn<sup>2</sup>†''' (yėrn), ''v.'' [Also ''earn''; prob. an altered form, due to confusion with ''yearn''<sup>1</sup>, with which it is generally merged, of *''erm'', < ME. ''ermen'', grieve, vex, < AS. ''yrman'', also ''ge-yrman'' (whence perhaps ''yearn'', as distinguished from ''earn'', like ''yean'' as distinguished from ''ean''), grieve, vex, < ''earm'' = D. G. ''arm'' = Icel. ''armr'' = Dan. Sw. ''arm'' = Goth. ''arms'', poor, miserable.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To grieve; mourn; sorrow.
{{smaller block|<poem>Falstaff he is dead,
And we must ''yearn'' therefore.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 3. 6.}}
<poem>Some of those French . . .
Assay the English carriages to burn,
Which to defend them scarcely had a man. . . .
Those ''yearning'' cries, that from the carriage came,
His blood yet hot, more highly doth inflame.</poem>
{{right|''Drayton'', Battle of Agincourt, st. 299.}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' To grieve; trouble; vex.
{{smaller block|It ''yearns'' my heart to hear the wench misconstrued.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Coxcomb, v. 3.}}
<poem>Nor care I who doth feed upon my cost;
It ''yearns'' me not if men my garments wear.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iv. 3. 26.}}
Alas, poor wretch! how it ''yearns'' my heart for him!
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Bartholomew Fair, iv. 4.}}}}

'''yearn<sup>3</sup>''' (yėrn), ''v. t.'' [A form of ''earn''<sup>1</sup>, simulating ''yearn''<sup>1</sup>, ''yearn''<sup>2</sup>, etc.] Same as ''earn''<sup>1</sup>. [Provincial or vulgar.]

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>My due reward, the which right well I deeme
I ''yearned'' have.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. vii. 15.}}}}
{{smaller block|She couldn’t afford to pay for schooling, and told me I must look out and ''yearn'' my own living while I was a mere chick.
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, I. 397.}}}}

'''yearn<sup>4</sup>''' (yėrn), ''v.'' [A var. of ''earn''<sup>4</sup>, or < ME. ''ȝe-ernen'', < AS. ''geyrnan'', run together: see ''earn''<sup>4</sup>, ''run''<sup>1</sup>.] Same as ''earn''<sup>2</sup>.
{{smaller block|His Honour the Duke will accept ane of our Dunlop cheeses, and it sall be my faut if a better was ever ''yearned'' in Lowden.
{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xxxix.}}}}

'''yearn<sup>5</sup>''' (yėrn), ''n.'' A dialectal (Scotch) form of ''earn''<sup>3</sup>.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye cliffs, the haunts of sailing ''yearns!''</poem>
{{right|''Burns'', On Capt. Matthew Henderson.}}}}

'''yearnful†''' (yėrn′fu̇l), ''a.'' [Also ''yernful, ernful''; < ''yearn''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ful''.] Mournful; distressing.
{{smaller block|Ala, Ala, was their ''yernfull'' note; their foode was the peoples almes.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 628.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>But, oh musicke, as in joyfull tunes, thy mery notes I did borrow,
So now lend mee thy ''yernfull'' tunes, to utter my sorrow.</poem>
{{right|''Damon and Pith.'', Old Plays, I. 195. (''Nares.'')}}}}

'''yearning<sup>1</sup>''' (yėr′ning), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ȝernynge''; verbal n. of ''yearn''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] The feeling of one who yearns; a strong feeling of tenderness, pity, or longing desire.
{{smaller block|All the herte festenede in the ''ȝernynge'' of Ihesu es turned in-to the fyre of lufe.
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 2.}}}}
